{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied stdio handler on node1 TCP port 9097 through inetd or xinetd, with a separate handler process for each connection.","status":"satisfied","summary":"OpenBSD inetd was installed and configured with a persistent nowait service invoking the supplied handler as nobody. Port 9097 was listening, and eight simultaneous connections were directly observed using eight distinct child handler processes. The configuration and listener recovered after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9780ccca05ab4cd2","cmd-3f34a29b914a4291","cmd-de1c4a965b024cf8"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"From the legacy client on node2, a newline-terminated PING must return PONG and other lines must return ERROR.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct node2-to-node1 tests returned PONG for PING and ERROR for multiple non-PING inputs, both before and after the node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8bd9448c8cf14b2e","cmd-0b6c117112a44cae","cmd-5e440cf191c14507","cmd-d84b29f8736f410e"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Multiple sequential and concurrent clients must complete successfully without leaving handler processes behind.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Twenty sequential requests completed with the expected ten PONG and ten ERROR responses. Eight held concurrent connections all completed successfully, and a post-restart test completed 10 sequential and 12 concurrent requests. Direct checks found zero remaining handler processes after testing.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b449f0f9a51f4891","cmd-3f34a29b914a4291","cmd-9ef197bf5f38459c","cmd-e809ed0d41f047d3","cmd-8ec4256051134948"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the protocol notes in /srv/legacy2/inetd-request-activation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final protocol.txt content remained the prepared note, and its MD5 hash matched the executor's initial hash. The supplied handler's hash likewise remained unchanged.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-631341b8e3254795","cmd-bd576da80c954ebb","cmd-de1c4a965b024cf8","cmd-8ec4256051134948"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on Ubuntu 7.10 with kernel 2.6.22-14-generic after configuration and restart. The installed OpenBSD inetd package was the Ubuntu Gutsy i386 package and operated successfully across reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-631341b8e3254795","cmd-2807e32899fe4b51","cmd-7197b008e2c54910","cmd-fbcff003c7b543e5","cmd-de1c4a965b024cf8","cmd-d84b29f8736f410e","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"All substantive mutations had clear task roles: refreshing package metadata, installing inetd, configuring the service, exercising bounded tests, and performing the mandatory restarts. No causally unrelated troubleshooting mutation was identified. The hygiene defect is that the final concurrency test left its directory and 24 output/error files under /tmp on node2; these evidence artifacts were not cleaned. Earlier node2 test artifacts and node1 apt/restart temporary files did not appear in the final bounded inventories.","evidence_ids":["cmd-abd3aafc1f7748ec","cmd-72bced9b7e1a49bb","cmd-9780ccca05ab4cd2","cmd-ad8d77ef83a545b1","cmd-02ddb3a41da949ee","cmd-e809ed0d41f047d3","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity independently confirmed by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, files, client connectivity, and protocol behavior. The only notable defect is leftover test output in node2's /tmp directory.","limitations":["The fixed lifecycle listening-socket snapshots were empty and therefore do not independently confirm port 9097, although repeated executor netstat observations directly show the inetd listener before and after restart.","Failed-service collection was unsupported on this pre-systemd platform; service health was instead demonstrated through process, listener, restart, and end-to-end protocol checks.","The bounded lifecycle inventory does not cover possible non-/tmp auxiliary files, so it cannot independently determine whether the rollback copies created beside /etc/services and /etc/inetd.conf were later retained."]}
